<BaseLayout title="Job Opportunity Engine — Governed career operations" description="A governed, local-first system for job collection, evaluation, and synthetic application preparation.">
  <article class="joe-case-study">
    <header class="joe-hero shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">Case study · private system</p>
      <h1>Job Opportunity<br />Engine</h1>
      <p class="joe-subtitle">Governed local-first system for job collection, evaluation, and application preparation.</p>
      <div class="joe-hero-grid">
        <p class="joe-summary">A career workflow should not treat a persuasive sentence as candidate truth. Job Opportunity Engine is a private, local-first system designed to keep evidence, decision rules, human authority, and external actions clearly separated.</p>
        <dl class="joe-facts"><div><dt>Role</dt><dd>System architecture, governance, requirements, and technical-program direction</dd></div><div><dt>Current state</dt><dd>Private Python + SQLite implementation with deterministic evaluation controls</dd></div></dl>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="joe-section joe-problem shell" aria-labelledby="problem-title"><div class="joe-section-label"><p class="eyebrow">01 · The problem</p></div><div class="joe-section-body"><h2 id="problem-title">Useful automation begins with a trustworthy record of reality.</h2><p class="joe-lead">AI can quickly recommend roles or draft application language. The difficult part is ensuring that a recommendation, match, or generated claim is grounded in verified information and remains subject to human judgment.</p><div class="joe-principles"><p>What job is actually being considered?</p><p>What can be supported by candidate evidence?</p><p>Which gaps are material—and which are learnable?</p><p>What may be prepared, and what must remain a human decision?</p></div></div></section>

    <section class="joe-section joe-role shell" aria-labelledby="role-title"><div class="joe-section-label"><p class="eyebrow">02 · Matthew’s role</p></div><div class="joe-section-body"><h2 id="role-title">Directing the system, not claiming the keyboard.</h2><p>Matthew Ralph defined the architecture, requirements, workflow states, evidence boundaries, decision models, validation criteria, and implementation direction. His role is that of a business systems and AI transformation operator: translating a consequential workflow into governed software and an inspectable operating model.</p><div class="authority-grid" aria-label="Authority boundary"><div><strong>Matthew</strong><span>Architecture, requirements, governance, validation</span></div><div><strong>AI coding agents</strong><span>Bounded implementation, inspection, testing, refinement</span></div><div><strong>Deterministic system</strong><span>Identity, evidence ownership, state, scoring, validation</span></div><div><strong>Human control</strong><span>Approval, claims, and external action</span></div></div><p class="joe-note">AI coding agents performed substantial bounded implementation, inspection, testing, and refinement under repository controls and human review. They did not independently decide what was true, what the system could authorize, or when an external action should occur.</p></div></section>

    <section class="joe-section joe-system shell" aria-labelledby="system-title"><div class="joe-section-label"><p class="eyebrow">03 · Current system model</p></div><div class="joe-section-body"><h2 id="system-title">A controlled path from intake to human review.</h2><p>The implemented core focuses on preserving context and decision traceability—not simulating an autonomous job-application agent.</p><div class="workflow-diagram" role="img" aria-label="Current implemented workflow from ATS or manual intake through human review, with broad web search and final submission marked as future"><div class="workflow-row"><span>ATS / manual intake</span><i>→</i><span>Store + normalize</span><i>→</i><span>Verify</span><i>→</i><span>Dedupe + state</span></div><div class="workflow-row"><span>Extract requirements</span><i>→</i><span>Evaluate against evidence</span><i>→</i><span>Classify gaps + capabilities</span><i>→</i><span>Score + prioritize</span></div><div class="workflow-row"><span>Synthetic application prep</span><i>→</i><span>Human review</span></div><div class="workflow-future"><span>Future, not current runtime</span><p>Broad external search automation · automated final submission</p></div></div></div></section>

    <section class="joe-section joe-governance shell" aria-labelledby="governance-title"><div class="joe-section-label"><p class="eyebrow">04 · Governance model</p></div><div class="joe-section-body"><h2 id="governance-title">AI proposes. Evidence proves. Humans approve.</h2><p>Candidate evidence is canonical and profile-isolated. Capability assessments may be proposed, but an interpretation becomes eligible for matching only when it is supported by linked evidence and approved by a human.</p><div class="evidence-diagram" role="img" aria-label="Evidence governance sequence"><div><b>1</b><strong>Career evidence</strong><span>Source material stays distinct from generated language.</span></div><div><b>2</b><strong>Governed assessment</strong><span>Capability interpretation is tied to appropriate evidence.</span></div><div><b>3</b><strong>Human approval</strong><span>Authority is explicit, rather than inferred from a model output.</span></div><div><b>4</b><strong>Eligible matching</strong><span>Only approved evidence may inform evaluation or preparation.</span></div></div></div></section>

    <section class="joe-section joe-decisions shell" aria-labelledby="decisions-title"><div class="joe-section-label"><p class="eyebrow">05 · Design decisions</p></div><div class="joe-section-body"><h2 id="decisions-title">The controls are the product.</h2><div class="decision-list"><article><span>01</span><h3>Generated output is not authoritative evidence</h3><p>A polished résumé bullet or application draft does not become true simply because a system generated it. Claims must remain traceable to governed evidence.</p></article><article><span>02</span><h3>Technical fluency is not software engineering</h3><p>The model distinguishes technical or program ownership from hands-on production implementation, preventing flattering but unsupported capability inflation.</p></article><article><span>03</span><h3>Application state and outcome are distinct</h3><p>A submission remains a submission even when a later outcome is rejection. Separating those facts protects lifecycle integrity and makes later analysis more meaningful.</p></article><article><span>04</span><h3>AI judgment and software authority are separated</h3><p>AI may assist interpretation. Deterministic controls preserve identity, evidence ownership, lifecycle state, validation, and critical decision rules.</p></article></div></div></section>

    <section class="joe-section joe-walkthrough shell" aria-labelledby="walkthrough-title"><div class="joe-section-label"><p class="eyebrow">06 · Synthetic walkthrough</p></div><div class="joe-section-body"><h2 id="walkthrough-title">A fictional example of depth-aware matching.</h2><p class="joe-note">This is an illustrative, fictional scenario. It is not a candidate record, application, or real job evaluation.</p><div class="depth-comparison"><article class="depth-match"><p class="eyebrow">Requirement A · program ownership</p><blockquote>“Lead deployment of agentic workflows across business operations. Partner with engineering and security. Establish governance, human-in-the-loop controls, evaluation frameworks, and operational readiness.”</blockquote><p><strong>Recognized concepts:</strong> Agentic Workflow Design · AI Governance · Human-in-the-Loop Design · Technical Program Direction</p><span class="depth-label">Technical fluency or program ownership</span></article><article class="depth-gap"><p class="eyebrow">Requirement B · hands-on implementation</p><blockquote>“Build distributed LLM inference services in production Python.”</blockquote><p><strong>Different classification:</strong> hands-on technical implementation. Systems or program evidence does not automatically establish software-engineering evidence.</p><span class="depth-label">Hands-on technical implementation</span></article></div></div></section>

    <section class="joe-section joe-validation shell" aria-labelledby="validation-title"><div class="joe-section-label"><p class="eyebrow">07 · Current state</p></div><div class="joe-section-body"><h2 id="validation-title">Implemented as a private, local-first foundation.</h2><div class="validation-grid"><div><strong>Python + SQLite</strong><span>Local persistence for jobs, postings, observations, evaluations, applications, artifacts, and events.</span></div><div><strong>Public checkpoint</strong><span>146 passing automated tests at the latest clean public checkpoint. It does not represent active private development.</span></div><div><strong>Private-data guard</strong><span>Repository-level policy check passed at the public checkpoint.</span></div><div><strong>Human authority</strong><span>Approval and final external action remain outside automated runtime scope.</span></div></div><aside class="evidence-links" aria-labelledby="joe-evidence-title"><p class="eyebrow">Public documentation</p><h3 id="joe-evidence-title">Inspect public evidence</h3><ul><li><a href="https://github.com/matt-rfs/matthew-ralph-work-samples/blob/main/job-opportunity-engine/synthetic-walkthrough.md">Synthetic walkthrough <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></li><li><a href="https://github.com/matt-rfs/matthew-ralph-work-samples/blob/main/job-opportunity-engine/design-decisions.md">Design decisions <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></li><li><a href="https://github.com/matt-rfs/matthew-ralph-work-samples/blob/main/job-opportunity-engine/validation-snapshot.md">Validation snapshot <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></li></ul></aside></div></section>

    <section class="joe-section joe-boundary shell" aria-labelledby="boundary-title"><div class="joe-section-label"><p class="eyebrow">08 · Scope boundary</p></div><div class="joe-section-body"><h2 id="boundary-title">Built for disciplined progress, not false autonomy.</h2><div class="scope-grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">Current</p><ul><li>Governed ATS and manual intake</li><li>Normalization, identity, verification, and deduplication</li><li>Evidence-bound evaluation and deterministic scoring</li><li>Capability matching and synthetic preparation</li></ul></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Not currently automated</p><ul><li>Broad web search</li><li>Autonomous browser application</li><li>Final application submission</li></ul></div></div><p class="joe-closing">The point is not to remove human responsibility from a consequential workflow. It is to make the information, choices, and boundaries that support human judgment more reliable.</p></div></section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
